## 1. Introduction
6-Mercaptopurine (6-MP), an anti-cancer drug, has been widely used for the treatment of various types of cancer, such as acute lymphoblastic leukemia and inflammatory bowel disease (Chen et al., 2013; Li et al., 2015). However, as a cytotoxic anti-cancer drug, 6-MP always brings about serious side effects (Kanemitsu et al., 2009). Therefore, the development of a simple and accurate quantitative method for the detection of 6-MP is of considerable importance.
To date, a series of analytical methods have been applied for the detection of 6-MP, including electrochemical (EC) methods (Zhou et al., 2012; Shahrokhian et al., 2012), chemiluminescence (CL) (Biparva et al., 2015), high-performance liquid chromatography (HPLC) (Li et al., 2016), mass spectrometry (MS) (Rosenfeld et al., 1977), and Raman spectroscopy (Han et al., 2014). Among these, EC assays are the most widely reported methods for the detection of 6-MP (Chen et al., 2013). However, the poor repeatability and complicated electrode modification process have limited the applications of such methods. CL assays have poor selectivity despite their high sensitivity. HPLC and MS are always used simultaneously to detect 6-MP. Although this approach is sensitive and selective, expensive equipment and complicated sample pretreatments are required. Raman assays also suffer from similar disadvantages as HPLC and MS. Therefore, an inexpensive, simple, sensitive, and accurate method for the detection of 6-MP is desired.
Semiconductor nanocrystalline quantum dots (QDs) have garnered considerable interest during the last two decades due to their high fluorescence quantum yields, size-dependent broad absorption, high extinction coefficients, and stability with respect to photobleaching (Wu et al., 2016; Wu et al., 2015; Cao et al., 2013). CdTe QDs have been developed for the sensitive determination of 6-MP based on the coordination effect between 6-MP and ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ (Gao et al., 2013). However, poor selectivity was inescapable because other thiol-containing substances such as cysteine (Cys) and glutathione (GSH) could interfere with the determination of 6-MP. Thus, the establishment of a highly selective method for the detection of 6-MP poses a challenge for analytical chemists.
Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are a relatively new family of nanoporous crystalline materials, in which transition metal ions or clusters are connected via polyfunctional organic ligands (Liu et al., 2013; Zhang et al., 2015). Due to their outstanding characteristics including infinite tunability, structural diversity, and excellent thermal stability, MOFs have been widely exploited for various applications such as gas storage, heterogeneous catalysis, conductivity, and drug delivery and sensing (He et al., 2014; Chughtai et al., 2015; Sun et al., 2016; Sun et al., 2013; Hao and Yan, 2015). Recently, Sun et al. (2015) used MOF Fe-MIL- $88\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ for the detection of 6-MP and reported a detection limit of $1.17~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ . However, compared to fluorescent nanoparticles, such as gold nanoparticles (Chen et al., 2013) and CdTe QDs (Gao et al., 2013), the sensitivity was relatively low. Because the use of MOFs alone results in poor sensitivity, the development of highly sensitive methods for the detection of 6-MP is a worthwhile, yet challenging undertaking.
In order to increase the selectivity and sensitivity, ratiometric fluorescent (RF) sensors are utilized, in which analyte concentrations are determined by measuring the ratios of the emission at two wavelengths (Choi et al., 2013). Compared with single-channel detection methods, RF sensors possess a built-in correction factor that eliminates false signals originating from environmental effects (Zhuang et al., 2014), removing the undulation of excitation light intensity and probe concentration; therefore, they exhibit enhanced precision and accuracy (Wang et al., 2015).
Herein, we proposed an effective RF sensor for the detection of 6-MP by combining $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) MOF and MPA-CdTe QDs. The fluorescence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) could be quenched by 6-MP, while other thiol-containing substances such as Cys and GSH caused almost no interference. The fluorescence intensity of the MPA-CdTe QDs increased upon interaction with 6-MP. As such, a novel RF sensor with high selectivity and sensitivity towards 6-MP was obtained. To the best of our knowledge, this is the first report regarding the ratiometric detection of 6-MP.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Reagents and instruments
2-Aminoterephtalic acid ( $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC, $99\%$ ), glutathione (GSH, $98\%$ ), and cysteine (Cys, $99\%$ ) were purchased from Sigma (Shanghai, China). Aluminum chloride $\mathrm{(AlCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O,~97\%)}$ was purchased from Tianli Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Tianjin, China). N,N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $99.5\%$ was purchased from Fuyu Fine Chemical Co. Ltd. (Tianjin, China). Dimethyl ketone, cadmium chloride $\mathrm{\CdCl}_{2}$ , $99\%$ ), and sodium borohydride $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ , $96\%$ ) were purchased from Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Ltd. (Shanghai, China). 6-Mercaptopurine (6-MP, $98\%$ ) and 3-mercaptopropionic acid (MPA, $99\%$ ) were purchased from J&K Chemical Co. (Beijing, China). Sodium tellurite ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{TeO}}_{3}$ , $99.8\%$ ) was purchased from Sigma-Aldrich (St. Louis, MO). Trisodium citrate dehydrate $(99\%)$ was purchased from Tianjin Chemical Reagent Co. Ltd. (Tianjin, China). Lysine, threonine, tryptophan, histidine, L-phenylalanine, and alanine were purchased from GL Biochem (Shanghai) Ltd. All other reagents were of analytical reagent grade and were used as received without further purification. Aqueous solutions were prepared with ultrapure water (18.2 $\mathbf{M}\Omega\cdot\mathbf{cm})$ , which was purified using a Millipore Milli-Q system.
UV-vis adsorption spectra were obtained using a TU-1901 spectrophotometer (Puxi Analytic Instrument Ltd of Beijing, China). Fourier transform-infrared (FT-IR) spectra were recorded in the range of $400~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ to $4000~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ on a Tensor 27 spectrometer (Bruker, German). All fluorescence measurements were obtained on a PE fluorescence spectrophotometer (LS55, USA). Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded on a Rigaku MiniFlex 600 diffractometer using Cu Kα X-rays with a scanning range of $5\mathrm{-}50^{\circ}$ at a scanning speed of $6^{\circ}/\mathrm{min}.$ Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) images were obtained using an FEI Tecnai G2 F20 transmission electron microscope. An FEI Quanta 200 scanning electron microscope was used to obtain scanning electron microscopy (SEM) images at an accelerating voltage of $20\mathrm{KV}.$
## 2.2. Synthesis of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al)
The synthesis of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) was adapted from a hydrothermal method reported previously (Ahnfeldt et al., 2009). Briefly, $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) was synthesized by dissolving $0.58\mathrm{~g~AlCl_{3}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ and $0.44\textrm{g N H}_{2}$ -BDC in $20~\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water in a Teflon insert placed in an autoclave. The autoclave was maintained at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 5 h under static conditions. The resulting yellow product was filtered off and washed with acetone. In order to remove the unreacted $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC in the pores, as-synthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) was treated overnight with DMF at $150~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , then the powder was dried overnight in a vacuum oven at $130^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ to remove trapped DMF.
## 2.3. Synthesis of CdTe QDs
CdTe QDs were prepared using a procedure adapted from a one-pot synthetic method reported previously (Sheng et al., 2010; Long et al., 2013). First, 2 mmol $\mathrm{CdCl}_{2}{\cdot}2.5\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ was dissolved in 150 mL ultrapure water in a three-neck flask, followed by the instant addition of $104~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ MPA, $800~\mathrm{{mg}}$ trisodium citrate dihydrate, 0.4 mmol ${\mathrm{Na}}_{2}{\mathrm{TeO}}_{3}$ , and $200\ \mathrm{mg\NaBH_{4}}$ . Next, the $\mathsf{p H}$ was adjusted to 10.5 with vigorous stirring. The obtained CdTe precursor solution was refluxed at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for about $30~\mathrm{min}$ . After three successive precipitation steps with absolute ethyl alcohol, MPA-CdTe QDs with prominent fluorescence were acquired. All reactions were carried out under ambient atmospheric conditions.
2.4. Fluorescence of the nanohybrid system in the presence of 6-MP
In a typical experimental setup, $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) solution $(0.002~\mathrm{mg{\cdot}m l^{-1}},$ ) and a certain amount of 6-MP were added to a $1.5~\mathrm{mL}$ EP vial. After incubation for $40\ \mathrm{min}$ , $20~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ purified MPA-CdTe QDs $\mathrm{0.005\mg{\cdot}m l^{-1}}$ ) was added to the above mixture and the solution was diluted with phosphate buffer $\mathrm{(pH}6.5)$ to a final volume of $200~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ . Upon excitation at $335~\mathrm{nm}$ , the fluorescence was recorded from $370\mathrm{nm}$ to $570\mathrm{nm}$ for the detection of 6-MP.
## 3. Results and discussion
3.1. Characterization of $N H_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al)
3.1.1 Fourier transform infrared (FTIR) spectroscopy
The FTIR spectrum of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) is shown in Fig. 1A. Two sharp bands at 3386 and 3498 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ were assigned to the symmetric and asymmetric stretching vibrations of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ group, respectively. The typical vibrational bands in the range of $1400{-}1700\ \mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ exhibited the carbonyl groups coordinated with ${\mathrm{A}}1^{3+}$ and free DMF encapsulated within the porous structure. Specifically, two absorption peaks around 1500 and $1610\ \mathrm{\cm^{-1}}$ were assigned to the asymmetric stretching vibrations of the carbonyl group, whereas the peaks around 1400 and $1442~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ might be assigned to the symmetric stretching of the carbonyl group. Furthermore, an additional absorption band at 1670 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was attributed to the stretching vibration of the carbonyl group in DMF.
## $3.l.2X$ -ray diffraction
As shown in Fig. 1B, the XRD pattern of as-synthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) was similar to the simulated pattern and indicated that pure phases were obtained (Ahnfeldt et al., 2009). The structure of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) contains chains of $\upmu{-}\mathrm{OH}$ corner-sharing $\mathrm{AlO}_{6}$ -octa-hedra, which are interconnected by $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC molecules to form one-dimensional pores. The peak signals of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53 change depending on the guest molecules inside the pores owing to breathing effects. The peaks at $8.8^{\circ}$ and $10.4^{\circ}$ corresponded to the as-synthesized $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al), in which unreacted $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -BDC was trapped in the structure (Seoane et al., 2013).
## 3.1.3 Transmission electron microscopy
Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) showed that $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) exhibited a regular rhomboid structure with a particle size of around $1\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Fig. S1A).
## 3.1.4 Spectral characteristics
The fluorescence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) was measured at room temperature. The excitation spectrum of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) showed a maximum emission peak at $425\mathrm{nm}$ when excited at $335\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2A). The bright blue luminescence of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) could be observed by the naked eye upon excitation with UV light at $365\mathrm{nm}$ (Fig. 2A, inset).
3.2. Characterization of CdTe QDs
## 3.2.1. Spectral characteristics
As shown in Fig. 2B, MPA-CdTe QDs displayed two excitation peaks around $335\mathrm{nm}$ and $385\mathrm{nm}$ , and the emission wavelength was centered at $528\ \mathrm{nm}$ . The purified QD solution exhibited very intense bright yellow green luminescence under UV light at 365 nm (Fig. 2B, inset). The fluorescence emission spectrum was very narrow and symmetric, suggesting that the obtained MPA-CdTe QDs were monodisperse and homogenous. In addition, the UV/Vis absorption spectrum of MPA-CdTe QDs (Fig. S2) revealed an absorption band at ${\sim}500\mathrm{nm}$ .
## Fig. 2 here
## 3.2.2. Transmission electron microscopy
TEM revealed that MPA-CdTe QDs were nearly spherical and slightly aggregated (Fig. S1B). The diameter of the MPA-CdTe QDs nanoparticles was estimated to be $\sim3\ \mathrm{nm}$ , which was consistent with the results obtained using the empirical formula (Yu et al., 2003).
3.3. Optimization of experimental conditions
The experimental conditions for the detection of 6-MP was optimized to obtain a high sensitivity. Because the pH of the reaction solution has a significant influence on the RF sensor, variations in the fluorescence intensity of the RF sensor were investigated over the $\mathsf{p H}$ range of 6.0 to 9.0. Upon analysis of the ratio of the fluorescence intensity as a function of $\mathrm{pH}$ , a relationship between the $(\mathrm{I_{0}/I})$ value and pH over a range of 6.0-9.0, where $\mathrm{I_{0}}=(\mathrm{I}_{425}/\mathrm{I}_{528})_{0}$ and $\mathrm{{I}}=(\mathrm{{I_{425}}/\mathrm{{I_{528}}}})$ the ratios of the fluorescence intensity of the MOF-CdTe QD nanohybrid system in the absence and presence of 6-MP, respectively, was observed. The ratio of the fluorescence intensity $(\mathrm{I_{0}/I})$ of the ratiometric probe increased below $\mathrm{pH}6.5$ and decreased as the $\mathrm{pH}$ increased (Fig. S3). Therefore, $\mathrm{pH}6.5$ was selected as the optimal pH for the detection of 6-MP. The effect of reaction time on the fluorescence intensity of the ratiometric probe was also considered. I0/I increased during the first $40\ \mathrm{min}$ , and then stabilized (Fig. S4), indicating that the reaction was complete within $40~\mathrm{min}$ ; therefore, a reaction time of 40 min was utilized in subsequent experiments.
## 3.4. Calibration curves and detection limit
The dose response of the sensing system towards 6-MP was examined in detail under the optimized experimental conditions. Upon addition of various concentrations of 6-MP (Fig. 3A), the intensity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) decreased continuously with increasing concentrations of 6-MP, while the fluorescence intensity of the CdTe QDs increased. As shown in Fig. 3B, a good linear relationship between $\mathrm{I_{0}/I}$ and the concentration of 6-MP within the range of $0{-}50~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ was observed. The linear curve was fitted with the regression equation $\mathrm{(I_{0}/I)}=1.150+0.083\mathrm{C_{6.MP}}\mathrm{(R}^{2}=0.9937\mathrm{)}$ and a detection limit of $0.15\upmu\mathrm{M}$ was determined at $\mathrm{S}/\mathrm{N}=3$ .
In order to confirm the sensitivity of the RF sensor, the dual emission sensor was compared to a single emission probe where $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) alone was used for the detection of 6-MP. With the single emission probe, the emission intensity of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) was quenched continuously with increasing concentrations of 6-MP (Fig. S5A). A linear relationship between the quenching ratio $\mathrm{F_{0}{-}F/F_{0}}$ and concentration of 6-MP in the range of $0{-}40~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ was obtained and the equation of linear regression was $\mathrm{F_{0^{-}F}}/\mathrm{F_{0}}=0.0074+0.011\mathrm{C_{6\mathrm{-}M P}}(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9913)$ with a detection limit of $1.17~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ at an S/N ratio of 3 $\mathrm{F}_{0}$ and $\mathrm{~F~}$ are the fluorescence intensity of the $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) in the absence and presence of 6-MP, respectively). Simultaneously, a single emission probe using CdTe QDs alone was also investigated for the detection of 6-MP (Fig. S5B). The fluorescence intensity of CdTe QDs increased continuously with increasing concentrations of 6-MP. A linear regression equation of $\mathrm{F–F_{0}}/\mathrm{F_{0}}\mathrm{=}0.1178+0.042\mathrm{C_{6-MP}}(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9931)$ was obtained with a detection limit of $0.46~\upmu\mathrm{M}$ ( $\mathrm{\tilde{F}_{0}}$ and F are the fluorescence intensity of the CdTe QDs in the absence and presence of 6-MP, respectively). Obviously, the sensitivity of the RF sensor was approximately 8-fold greater than that of the single emission probe comprised of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) alone and 3-fold greater than that comprised of CdTe QDs alone. Thus, compared with single-emission sensors, RF sensors can significantly increase the sensitivity for the detection of 6-MP.
Compared with other reported methods for 6-MP detection (Table S1), the detection limit of the RF sensor was much lower than some other measurement protocols such as capillary electrophoresis (Wang et al., 2000), surface enhanced Raman scattering (Fu et al., 2014), and Fe-MIL- $88\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ MOF luminescent method (Sun et al., 2015). Although the detection limit was higher than that of a gold nanoparticle-based fluorescence switch sensor (Chen et al., 2013), the selectivity was largely improved, and thus the RF sensor may have promising applications.
