## 3.5. Sensing selectivity
According to previous reports, GSH and Cys can enhance the fluorescence intensity of AuNPs and CdTe QDs (Su et al., 2015; Wang et al., 2009; Rodrigues et al., 2014). However, in previous reports regarding AuNPs and CdTe QDs for the detection of 6-MP (Gao et al., 2013; Chen et al., 2013; Shen et al., 2006), thiol-containing substances such as GSH and Cys were not investigated as interfering substances . In order to determine the selectivity of the developed MOF-CdTe QD nanohybrid system towards 6-MP, some common molecules and amino acids such as glucose, fructose, galactose, lysine, threonine, tryptophan, etc. were investigated. As shown in Fig. 4, only GSH and Cys had a certain effect on this system. In order to demonstrate the higher selectivity of the RF sensor than that of the single emission CdTe QD probe, interference by GSH and Cys (each $20\upmu\mathrm{M},$ ) inthe detection of same concentration 6-MPwas investigated. The errors caused by GSH and Cys were $42.01\%$ and $30.32\%$ , respectively, for the single emission CdTe QD probe, while the errors caused by GSH and Cys were $21.25\%$ and $12.36\%$ for the RF sensor.
Due to the dual-emission of the developed ratiometric sensor, the interference by GSH and Cys was largely reduced, resulting in an improved selectivity toward 6-MP. Overall, high selectivity and sensitivity were achieved with the developed RF sensor.
## Fig. 4 here
## 3.6. Stability of RF sensor
To investigate the stability of the RF sensor, the repeatability (intra-day) of the assay was evaluated using seven measurements during a day, and intermediate precision (inter-day) of the assay was evaluated over seven days in the absence and presence of 6-MP $(20~\upmu\mathrm{M})$ , respectively (Fig. S6). The intra- and inter-day RSDs were $0.6\%$ and $1.4\%$ for the blank sample, and $1.2\%$ and $2.1\%$ for the 6-MP-containing sample. These results indicated that the RF sensor has of very good stability and repeatability.
## 3.7. Determination of 6-MP in human urine
In order to study the reliability of the present assay under real conditions, the proposed sensor was used to detect 6-MP in human urine samples. The urine sample obtained from a healthy volunteer was diluted 50-fold with doubly distilled water and directly used for experimental test. Satisfactory recoveries in the range of $102.4\%$ to $104.6\%$ were obtained with the RSDs of $2.9\%$ to $4.4\%$ (Table S2). Therefore, the analytical performance of the RF sensor offers a promising alternative for accurate measurements of 6-MP in real samples.
## 3.8. Mechanism of fluorescent sensing
The fluorescence quenching of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) in the presence of 6-MP was proposed to be due to strong inner filter effects (IFEs) between $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) and 6-MP. IFEs result from the absorption of the excitation and/or emission of the fluorescent substance by other absorbents in the detection system (Chen and Ren, 2012; Li et al., 2013; Shang and Dong, 2009). Fig. 5A shows the overlaps between the excitation spectrum of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) and absorption spectra of 6-MP and other thiol-containing substances, such as GSH and Cys. A large spectral overlap between $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) ( $332~\mathrm{nm})$ and 6-MP $322~\mathrm{nm}$ ) was observed. Owing to the strong absorption of 6-MP, the excitation light of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) is weakened. Thus, the fluorescence quenching in the presence of 6-MP can be attributed to fluorescence IFE. In general, greater spectral overlap between the absorbance spectrum of the analyte and the excitation spectrum of the sensor leads to higher IFEs (Chang and Ho, 2015). In this case, the peak at $332{\mathrm{nm}}$ has negligible overlap with those of GSH and Cys; therefore, the excitation light for $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al) was hardly weakened. Thus, GSH and Cys caused almost no interference in the determination of 6-MP. To further verify the sensing mechanism, UV-vis absorption spectra were obtained upon incremental addition of 6-MP to $\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ -MIL-53(Al). A significant and rapid signal enhancement was observed upon gradual increase of the amount of 6-MP (Fig. 5B). Furthermore, the fluorescence enhancement mechanism was investigated in order to understand the interaction between the CdTe QDs and 6-MP to generate the enhanced fluorescence of the QDs. 6-MP contains a thiol functional group (Fig. S7). In order to determine whether the thiol group binds to the surface of the CdTe QDs, FTIR measurements were carried out. A band at 2677 $\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was assigned to the 6-MP thiol (S-H) stretching vibration (Fig. S8). Upon interaction of 6-MP with CdTe QDs, the band at $2677~\mathrm{{cm}^{-1}}$ disappeared. Because the thiol group has a high affinity for heavy metal ions (Rodrigues et al., 2014), it can be assumed that the most probable reaction mechanism involves the complexation of incompletely bound cadmium ions ( $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ surface traps) on the QD surface by the thiol group of 6-MP (Frigerio et al., 2012). Electronic defects or dangling bonds are crystal imperfections, which are localized on the surface (Hayes et al., 1989). These traps produce energy states that facilitate nonradiative recombination and lead to a weaker quantum yield of the QD nanoparticles (Woggon, 1997). The thiol group of 6-MP binds to these sites effectively removing the trap states (Dannhauser et al., 1986), which improves the passivation of the QDs and increases the intensity of the photoluminescence emission.
## 4. Conclusion
In summary, a dual-emission RF sensor based on the hybridization of $\mathrm{NH}_{2}.$ -MIL-53(Al) and MPA-CdTe QDs was designed for the detection of 6-MP. The proposed sensor exhibited a higher detection sensitivity and selectivity as compared to conventional, single emission-based sensors. Moreover, the developed RF sensor was successfully used for the detection of 6-MP in human urine with satisfactory results. Therefore, the present RF sensor can be regarded as a potential detection method for 6-MP sensing.
